2026-10-04T02:54:39.2651457Z     Received string:      "### 1.1 Our own database dumps (operator-held copies)
2026-10-04T02:54:39.2651687Z 
2026-10-04T02:54:39.2652957Z     **Fact (repo).** `docs/deploy-runbook.md` §2 step 3 / §3 tells the operator to `pg_dump` a reference copy before any deploy with a migration, and store it \"somewhere durable (1Password vault attachment, S3 bucket, etc.)\". The business continuity plan template also proposes a weekly manual export. These are full copies of personal data. **Nothing ages them out automatically.**
2026-10-04T02:54:39.2653919Z 
2026-10-04T02:54:39.2654237Z     **Procedure (ADOPTED by the owner 2026-10-03, O-611-4; the 30-day and 90-day limits are published on the policies):**
2026-10-04T02:54:39.2654777Z     - Every dump is named with its date and stored only in one owner-controlled location.
2026-10-04T02:54:39.2655203Z     - Delete a dump 30 days after the deploy it was taken for is verified.
2026-10-04T02:54:39.2655814Z     - Never keep any dump for more than 90 days. That bound alone keeps the six-month promise, even if a dump is taken the day before a deletion request.
2026-10-04T02:54:39.2656903Z     - On the first working day of each month, the owner lists the stored dumps and deletes any older than these limits. The check goes in the quarterly review log (`docs/soc2/runbook-quarterly-review.md`).
2026-10-04T02:54:39.2657687Z     - A dump is never copied to a laptop disk outside the vault. If one is, it is deleted the same day.
